<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#russification">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Russification</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Russification was the policy of spreading the Russian language, culture and administration among the non-Russian peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. It intensified under Alexander III from the 1880s, when Russian replaced German and other languages in the schools and courts of the Baltic provinces, and from 1899 it threatened the autonomy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>. Soviet policies revived it from the 1930s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russification.jpg" alt="Russification" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Da voli · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Russification_evidence.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Russification is a form of cultural assimilation in which non-Russians adopt Russian culture and the Russian language, either voluntarily or as a result of deliberate state policy. The governments of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> pursued it, either as a goal in itself or as a consequence of centralisation and modernisation. In politics it can mean assigning Russians to lead administrative posts in national institutions; in culture it consists chiefly of the dominance of Russian in official business. Some researchers distinguish Russification, a change of one's ethnic identity to Russian, from Russianization, the spread of Russian language, culture and people, and from Sovietization.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The assimilation of Volga-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnic-peoples">Finnic peoples</a> such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merya">Merya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muroma">Muroma</a> began early in the 2nd millennium CE, with the eastward expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-slavs">East Slavs</a>. After the defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> in 1856 and the January Uprising of 1863, Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a> increased Russification to reduce the threat of rebellion. In Congress Poland the Polish and Belarusian languages were banned in public places in 1864 and Polish in schools in the 1880s, and a secret Polish education network, including the Flying University, arose in response. The Ems Ukaz of 1876 banned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> in Ukrainian.</p><p>Under Alexander III an ukaz of 14 September 1885 made Russian mandatory for officials in the Baltic governorates, and by the early 1890s it was the language of instruction in their schools. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, Tartu University shifted from German to Russian in 1893. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, Russification between 1899 and 1905 and from 1908 to 1917 aimed at ending the country's autonomy, and Finnish opposition was one of the main factors behind its declaration of independence in 1917.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the 1917 revolution the Soviet authorities abolished the Arabic alphabet for native languages in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> region, replacing it with one based on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet until the late 1930s. In 1939 and 1940 a number of these languages, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a>, Kazakh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> and Azerbaijani, were moved to the Cyrillic script, with spelling that had to follow Russian. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the northern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a> became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaliningrad-oblast">Kaliningrad Oblast</a>, whose German population was expelled and replaced by Russians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarusians">Belarusians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a>. Some historians who regard the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> as a colonial empire have applied to it the phrase prison of nations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Russification” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Russification</b> bore on religion as well as language: the Catholic Church was persecuted, most monasteries were closed and nationalised, papal bulls could not be read without the government's consent, and in 1875 the Uniate Eparchy of Chełm-Belz was united with the Russian Orthodox Church.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Prikaz of the Kazan Palace undertook the forced <b>Russification</b> and Christianization of the Tatars and other peoples, and the term Tsardom of Kazan was used until 1708.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khanate-of-kazan">Khanate of Kazan · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the nineteenth century a national movement promoted the Finnish language and culture, inspired by the epic Kalevala, compiled by Elias Lönnrot, while <b>Russification</b> policies from 1899 provoked resistance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Nationality policy fluctuated between the promotion of local languages and elites in the 1920s, deportations of whole peoples such as the Chechens and Crimean Tatars in the 1940s, and <b>Russification</b> later.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After Stalin, Aleksey Kirichenko became in 1953 the first ethnic Ukrainian to lead the party since the 1920s; Petro Shelest, removed in 1972, was followed by Volodymyr Shcherbytsky's <b>Russification</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ukrainian-ssr">Ukrainian SSR · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Courland knighthood kept control of local government, courts and the Lutheran church under the Baltic special order, and German remained the language of administration until the <b>Russification</b> of the 1880s.</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-courland">Governorate of Courland</a></li></ul></section>
